Boudewijn Waijers wrote:
> Jakob Creutzig wrote:
> they have a chance of eating eachother, thus reducing their number.

OOhhh, nice. Puddings could merge as well as split. Say, any pair of
same-type[1] puddings on adjacent squares has a 25% chance of merging
each turn. When they merge their HPs combine (pushing the max HP up
accordingly), so splitting them, healing them and then allowing them to
re-merge would quickly create extremely tough puddings.
Say we start with a single pudding at 50/50 HP. You split it into two,
which are at 25/50 each. You then split them further, so you have four
puddings at 12/50, 13/50, 12/50, 13/50. Then you leave the level for a
while, and when you come back they are all 50/50. They then merge into
a giant 200/200 pudding, or maybe just a pair of 100/100 ones.

Of course, a big pudding with lots of hit points is probably the exact
opposite of a pudding-farming-deterrent, so I would suggest that a
pudding's speed and ability to hit/hurt was directly related to their
max HP. Therefore, in the example above, your 200/200 mergee would be a
high-speed uber-pud capable of chomping through mithril like it was so
much wet newspaper for 60HP of damage a time. Or perhaps you'd rather
the other scenario, with a pair of slightly slower 100/100 puddings
doing 40 damage each. And that's just from a single pudding, split into
3 generations. Imagine what could spawn from the so-called "sea of
puddings" described elsewhere in this thread. Throw in a small chance
of spontaneous splitting (say 0.1% per turn for each sufficient healthy
maxhp 50 pudding, rising by 0.2% per 10 MaxHP) and the situation could
very quickly get out of control. You could find yourself frantically
battling a lead uber-pud that re-inforced itself from a vast,
slef-perpetuating mob behind it faster than you could knock the hit
points off.
Maybe when they get to 150 MaxHP they could become E-proof as well.
*Evil Grin*

Of course this is all very well for black puddings, but brown puddings
don't bite. Perhaps harmless browns could be given teeth when they
reach a certain size (say, 100 MaxHP).

Death-drop exploitation problem solved. Under this system, mass pudding
farming would still be possible, but on anything more than a fairly
inefficient, limited scale it would have the potential to become very
dangerous (albeit great fun) for even high-powered or careful players.

Of course the problem you'd have then would be players nurturing
massive 1000/1000 blobs of rolling death and casting 'charm monster',
but that's nethack for you. Besides, I think the 'spontaneous split'
described above would take care of that little problem, just as long as
the 'offspring' was not guaranteed to be as tame as the 'parent'. Oh,
and of course, a merge between a hostile and tame pudding has only a
50/50 chance of turning out hostile...


